{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The candidate protocol lacks a sample size, sampling distribution, history-item exclusion policy, tie rule and rank-delta convention. Repeated targets require explicit handling. These choices can materially change perturbation results.",
    "ModelView compatibility remains unverified: the demonstrated scoring interface accepts B×L features and B×L×2 candidates, while the proposal assumes final-feature candidate scoring. Padding audits also need supported sequence lengths, numerical tolerances and an explicit response to failure. Any recurrent-computation fix must remain consistent with the embedded predictor.",
    "Reproducibility controls are incomplete: specify batch/user limits, shuffle repetitions, popularity boundaries, metric aggregation and uncertainty estimates. Diagnostic sampling must preserve training RNG and loader state; model mode should be restored with try/finally.",
    "Training-sample perturbations can reflect memorization or unrealistic histories. Their relationship to validation gains remains untested, and the report provides no bounded decision rule connecting findings to a subsequent GRU4Rec intervention.",
    "Originality 4/10 is reasonable, but future potential 8/10 is speculative and difficulty 3/10 may underestimate interface and padding work. References need verified titles and directly supported claims."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Establish reproducible candidate and ranking rules, including repeated-item handling, without changing the pinned benchmark evaluator.",
      "query": "In sequential recommendation history-perturbation diagnostics using GRU4Rec, how do candidate sample size, negative-sampling distribution, exclusion of previously interacted items, repeated next-item targets and score ties affect paired target-rank changes and top-k overlap? Identify reproducible protocols distinguishing sampled diagnostic ranks from full-catalog ranking."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Resolve implementation risks behind the claim that the probes require no architecture or predictor changes.",
      "query": "For PyTorch GRU sequential recommenders with left-padded histories, what implementation and tests ensure padding-invariant recurrent outputs, independent hidden states across users, and correct final-nonpadding readout? Explain compacting and packing sequences, restoring outputs to original positions, numerical tolerances and consistent training/inference behavior."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether perturbation sensitivity can guide useful interventions and calibrate the proposal's potential rating.",
      "query": "What empirical research validates deletion and order-shuffling diagnostics for sequential recommendation against predictive accuracy improvements? Focus on matched oldest-versus-newest deletion, last-item-preserving shuffles, unrealistic perturbed histories, training memorization, paired user-level uncertainty and popularity/history-length stratification, especially for GRU4Rec."
    }
  ]
}
